.TH "COMPLYCTL_CELSCANNER\-PLUGIN" "7" "August 2025" "Complyctl CELScanner Plugin" ""
.SH NAME
complyctl\-celscanner\-plugin \- a plugin which extends the complyctl
capabilities to use CEL (Common Expression Language) for compliance
validation.
.SH DESCRIPTION
The plugin is not meant to be executed directly, it communicates with
complyctl via gRPC.
It has configurable options that can be configured via a manifest file,
complyctl processes the manifest file and sends the configuration values
to the plugin.
.PP
When the plugin receives the \f[B]generate\f[R] command from complyctl,
it will convert OSCAL rules from the assessment plan into CEL
expressions and generate rule files in YAML format.
The generated files are placed in the \f[B]celscanner\f[R] directory
under user workspace.
.PP
When the plugin receives the \f[B]scan\f[R] command from complyctl, it
will evaluate the CEL rules against the target environment using
multiple input sources (Kubernetes, filesystem, HTTP, system) and return
the observations to complyctl based on CEL evaluation results.
.PP
The plugin supports both local evaluation and remote evaluation via CEL
RPC Server.
For security reasons, system command execution is limited to service
status checks only.
.SH FILES
\f[B]/usr/share/complytime/plugins/celscanner\-plugin\f[R] Default
plugin binary location.
.PP
\f[B]/etc/complytime/config.d/c2p\-celscanner\-manifest.json\f[R]
Optional drop\-in manifest file with customized plugin configurations.
.PP
\f[B]\[ti]/complytime/celscanner/policy/cel\-rules.yaml\f[R] Generated
CEL rules file (assuming default workspace).
.PP
\f[B]\[ti]/complytime/celscanner/results/cel\-results.yaml\f[R] Scan
results file (assuming default workspace).
.SH FEATURES
\f[B]Input Sources:\f[R] \- Kubernetes: Query and validate Kubernetes
resources \- Filesystem: Check file contents and properties \- HTTP:
Make API calls for validation \- System: Limited to service status
checks only (systemctl, getenforce) for security
.PP
\f[B]Execution Modes:\f[R] \- Local Mode: Direct CEL evaluation in the
plugin process \- RPC Mode: Remote evaluation via CEL RPC Server
.PP
\f[B]Rule Management:\f[R] \- YAML\-based rule storage system \-
One\-to\-many mapping: One OSCAL RuleSet can map to multiple CEL rules
\- Custom mapping configuration support \- Hierarchical lookup by
RuleSet ID or Check ID
.SH EXAMPLES
The following steps demonstrate a typical workflow using the CELScanner
plugin for Kubernetes compliance validation.
.PP
Step 1: Create a manifest file with plugin configuration
.PP
$ cat > manifest.yaml <<EOF assessment\-plan: assessment\-plan.json
plugins: \- name: celscanner\-plugin config: workspace: ./workspace
enable_kubernetes: true target_name: production\-cluster
include_namespaces: default,production mapping_file: mappings.yaml EOF
.PP
Step 2: Generate CEL rules from the assessment plan
.PP
$ complyctl generate \-m manifest.yaml
.PP
This creates CEL rules in
\[ti]/complytime/celscanner/policy/cel\-rules.yaml.
.PP
Step 3: Scan the environment with the generated rules
.PP
$ complyctl scan \-m manifest.yaml
.PP
The scan results are saved to
\[ti]/complytime/celscanner/results/cel\-results.yaml.
.PP
Step 4: Generate a compliance report
.PP
$ complyctl report \-m manifest.yaml
.SH RULE MAPPING
The plugin uses a flexible mapping system to convert OSCAL rules to CEL
expressions.
Create a mapping file:
.PP
$ cat > mappings.yaml <<EOF version: \[lq]1.0\[rq] mappings: # Map to
stored rules sshd\-service: type: stored_rules rule_ids: \-
sshd\-service\-enabled \- sshd\-service\-running
.PP
# Map to inline rules pod\-security: type: inline rules: \- id:
pod\-security\-context expression:
\[lq]has(resource.spec.securityContext)\[rq] inputs: \- name: resource
type: kubernetes resource: pods EOF
.SH SYSTEM CHECKS
For security, system commands are restricted to: \- Service status:
\f[CR]systemctl is\-active <service>\f[R] \- Service enabled:
\f[CR]systemctl is\-enabled <service>\f[R] \- SELinux status:
\f[CR]getenforce\f[R]
.PP
Example:
.IP
.EX
sshd\-service\-enabled:
  expression: \[aq]service.success && contains(service.output, \[dq]enabled\[dq])\[aq]
  inputs:
    \- name: service
      type: system
      command: systemctl
      args: [is\-enabled, sshd]
.EE
